2019 0 obj
<<
/Title (Observations. Both formats gave identical data on the 10-record test. Here the binary file is bigger \(36 bytes per record because name[24] is always stored in full, against about 23 bytes per text line\), yet it is roughly 10 to 15 times faster to read and write because there is no number-to-text conversion. Updating the 50,000th record took about 0.01 ms with fseek \(O\(1\)\) against about 21 ms for rewriting the text file \(O\(n\)\), roughly 1,000 times slower. Times vary slightly from run to run and between computers.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1988 0 R
/Prev 2008 0 R
/Next 2020 0 R
>>
endobj
2020 0 obj
<<
/Title (Engineering trade-off. Binary files are not always portable: another computer or compiler may use a different int size, byte order \(endianness\) or structure padding, so the same bytes can be read wrongly. Text is portable and human-readable but slower and not updatable in place. Recommendation: use a text \(CSV-style\) file for donors \(variable length, shared between hospitals, easy to check\) and a fixed-record binary file for stock \(numbers only, small, updated very often\).)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1988 0 R
/Prev 2019 0 R
/Next 2009 0 R
>>
endobj
